UNITED STATES MISSION TO THE UNITED NATIONS
Office of Press and Public Diplomacy
FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE
January 27, 2025
Statement on International Holocaust Remembrance Day
Today, on International Holocaust Remembrance Day, we honor the memory of six million Jewish men, women, and children – and millions of others – systematically murdered by the Nazis. This solemn occasion compels us to reflect on one of the darkest chapters in human history and to reaffirm our commitment to the universal values of dignity and human rights.
This year’s commemoration reminds us of the catastrophic consequences of hatred, dehumanization, and indifference. It underscores our collective responsibility to ensure that the atrocities of the Holocaust are never repeated and to combat antisemitism wherever it exists.
We owe an immense debt to Holocaust survivors. Their courage in sharing their stories serves as a powerful reminder of the resilience of the human spirit and the importance of bearing witness. It is vital to preserve their testimonies and educate future generations about the Holocaust – to ensure its lessons are never forgotten.
Tragically, we live in a world where antisemitism and Holocaust denial are resurgent. Recent reports reveal alarming trends, including the proliferation of Holocaust distortion and antisemitic rhetoric online and in public discourse.
These insidious forms of hate must be met with unequivocal condemnation and decisive action. The United States stands firmly against these threats, endorsing the International Holocaust Remembrance Alliance’s (IHRA) Working Definition of Antisemitism and advocating for adoption of the Global Guidelines for Countering Antisemitism, endorsed by dozens of countries and multilateral organizations since being launched in July 2024.
As we mark the 80th anniversary this year of the end of World War II and the liberation of Nazi concentration and death camps, we are reminded that atrocities like the Holocaust are not inevitable; they happen when hatred spreads and good people remain silent. It is our duty to speak out against hate, to protect the vulnerable, and to uphold the principles of justice and human rights.
Let us honor the memory of those who perished by committing ourselves to building a world where such horrors are impossible. Let us stand united in the fight against antisemitism and all forms of hatred. And let us never waver in our resolve to uphold the promise of “Never Again.”
