Julie Lelek
Adviser
New York,New York
January 22, 2024
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Ambassador Danese, for convening today’s meeting.
The United States thanks the Minister of Foreign Affairs, International Cooperation and Communities of the Republic of Guinea-Bissau, Mr. Carlos Pinto Pereira, for his presentation.
We would further like to thank Ms. Freeman, Ms. Boutin, and Ms. Cabral dos Santos Indeque for their presentations and contributions to today’s discussion.
Colleagues, Guinea-Bissau was one of the first countries in the world to fulsomely engage with the Peacebuilding Commission.
Its continued engagement with the PBC and the PBF for close to two decades is indicative of the beneficial relationships that have flourished over that time.
We support Guinea-Bissau’s and the Peacebuilding Fund’s focus on political dialogue, the fight against drugs and transnational crime, and support for human rights. This is especially important given that these vital areas were ones which the former UN Integrated Peacebuilding Office in Guinea-Bissau (UNIOGBIS) supported.
We encourage stakeholders to work together to specify a date for presidential and legislative elections in Guinea-Bissau as soon as feasible.
In this vein, we look forward to the timely visit of the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) high-level political mission announced in the December Heads of State Final Communique.
The United States also commends the efforts of regional and sub-regional organizations on peacebuilding efforts in West Africa writ large, including the African Union, the Economic Community of Central African States (ECCAS) and ECOWAS.
Guinea-Bissau is clearly committed to improving development cooperation with the UN and other partners. This includes its important partnerships with the World Bank, the International Monetary Fund, the African Development Bank and the Islamic Development Bank.
We further welcome Guinea-Bissau’s partnerships with Civil Society Organizations, including the Citizens’ Convention. The Conventions’ expertise on critical issues such as health, youth, employment, inclusion, and human rights can greatly assist Guinea-Bissau in sustaining peace efforts.
In closing, we join stakeholders, including those present today, in urging further efforts by Guinea-Bissau in governance, transparency, and economic diversification to promote growth and peace. Thank you.
