Jonathan Shrier
Deputy Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
January 17, 2024
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Ambassador Danese, for convening today’s meeting.
We would also like to thank the Permanent Representatives of Guatemala, Honduras, and Ecuador for their briefings. Insights from Guatemala, Honduras, and Ecuador are critical to informing the Commission’s work.
Thank you also USG Rider and ASG Spehar for your valuable comments, including, in particular your, recognition of the value of working with international financial institutions.
We would also like to thank Mr. Ilan Goldfajn, President of the Inter-American Development Bank, for his presentation.
The United States commends the Inter-American Development Bank on its inaugural Framework for Supporting Populations in Situations of Fragility, Conflict, and Criminal Violence.
Indeed, the Framework represents clear acknowledgment that fragility, conflict, and criminal violence severely affect social and economic development of millions for people across Latin America and the Caribbean.
The Inter-American Development Bank now joins other multilateral banks, including the African and Asian Development Banks, in highlighting the role of multilateral development banks as “agents for peace”.
In particular, we welcome the Framework’s emphasis on knowledge and data. The United States encourages further knowledge sharing with and across donors, other multilateral development banks, and the UN, for a common understanding of the problem set in various contexts.
We also welcome the Framework’s emphasis on context specific and adaptable approaches to programs, and recognition of pockets of territorial and subnational fragility.
We further encourage joint and complementary programming in fragile and conflict-affected contexts. Focusing such programs on infrastructure, governance, and social resilience are critical to supporting peace.
Additionally, the United States recommends that programs should prioritize investment in locally-led mechanisms, which we find to be the most effective.
Overall, we find the Framework practical and actionable, and welcome its implementation.
In closing, we welcome the progress we have made as an international community in elevating more strategic, deliberate approaches to conflict prevention and peacebuilding efforts.
Thank you.
