Faith Kroeker-Maus
Economic Adviser
New York, New York
January 17, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Good morning, excellencies and colleagues.
We thank Spain and Costa Rica for taking on the leadership positions as co-facilitators to develop these terms of reference.
The United States is a strong supporter of an open, inclusive, and transparent process to implement the Global Digital Compact, with the active participation of all stakeholders, including the private sector, civil society, and academic and research institutions. It is imperative that the process is transparent and participatory for those outside government.
From a substantive standpoint, we recommend that these terms of reference build on the progress made in the General Assembly in 2024, including the adoption of the first standalone resolution on AI in March 2024 and the GDC during the Summit of the Future.
As many have reiterated throughout the GDC process, we need to avoid duplication of effort, and the ACABQ has already flagged this concern on AI during December’s budget discussions.
For the Independent Scientific Panel, we should avoid duplicating the work of existing multidisciplinary networks related to AI. We continue to believe the panel will be best served by leveraging a truly independent, scientific, and multidisciplinary group of experts.
Furthermore, we heard suggestions to replicate the IPCC model during the GDC negotiation. We reiterate our view that this analogy is misguided given how dramatically AI differs as a policy area from climate change.
As for the Dialogue, we recall that its purpose is to facilitate multistakeholder discussion. Therefore, instead of creating a new initiative, we recommend leveraging existing multistakeholder fora – like the Internet Governance Forum and/or other UN instruments related AI, as identified by the UN Inter-Agency Working Group on Artificial Intelligence.
Consultative, multistakeholder, and multidisciplinary approaches to policy making are integral to U.S. policy development, including the development
of solutions to manage the risks and benefits of AI at home and abroad. We strongly believe the international community will benefit from these approaches.
Finally, we underscore the importance of keeping these two workstreams separate, timebound, and with discrete secretariats providing support to member states.
The United States looks forward to working with all stakeholders to accelerate progress on achieving the Sustainable Development Goals.
