Jonathan Shrier
U.S. Deputy Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
January 15, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Ambassador Danese for convening today’s meeting.
The United Staes would also like to thank Ambassador Kamau, Chair of the Peacebuilding Fund Advisory Group, and the two presenters from the Advisory Group, as well as Assistant Secretary-General for Peacebuilding Support Elizabeth Spehar for their presentations.
We would also like to thank the Ambassador of Chad for his briefing. The insights shared and lessons learned from experiences in Chad are valuable to other PBF recipient countries, as well as to the Commission’s work writ large.
Colleagues, understanding how the PBF affects peacebuilding efforts will help inform member states’ approach to this year’s Peacebuilding Architecture Review, including resourcing and financing.
As experience has demonstrated, modest investments can yield — and have yielded — transformational impacts in supporting post-conflict environments. Yet, peacebuilding efforts need more predictable and increased investments.
We are pleased the General Assembly approved assessed contributions for the PBF of $50 million annually for a more predictable baseline of resources, which will allow the PBF to do its vital work.
The assessed contributions are a start, but they are not sufficient for the tremendous needs that exist around the world. Thus, voluntary contributions to the PBF remain essential.
The Secretary-General’s updated Terms of Reference for the Peacebuilding Fund preserves the flexibility and adaptability needed for the Fund to deliver, while ensuring that member states have the requisite level of oversight.
We further welcome the Peacebuilding Support Office’s efforts to highlight specific results through enhanced monitoring, particularly in individual country settings. These measures aid in retaining member state support and investment.
In addition, the Pact for the Future calls for the establishment of more systematic and strategic partnerships between the Commission and stakeholders, including international financial institutions.
These efforts by the PBF and new partnerships can help mobilize financing for sustaining peace and help to align national development, peacebuilding and prevention approaches.
Additionally, and consistent with General Assembly resolution 76/305 on Financing for Peacebuilding, the United States supports more coordination across peacebuilding, development, and humanitarian efforts.
For example, the UN-World Bank Humanitarian-Development-Peacebuilding and Partnership (HDPP) facility can further drive coherence and positively affect peacebuilding efforts between these organizations. We encourage further partnerships like this.
In closing, the United States will continue to support UN peacebuilding efforts, including through the Peacebuilding Fund. We encourage others
to take concrete steps to support and resource this vital work. Thank you.
