United States Mission to the United Nations
Office of Press and Public Diplomacy
For Immediate Release
January 14, 2025
The below is attributable to U.S. Mission to the United Nations Spokesperson Nate Evans:
Today, the United States, in partnership with co-sponsors Australia, Austria, Canada, Estonia, Finland, France, Japan, Latvia, Lithuania, the Netherlands, Norway, Poland, the Republic of Korea, Sweden, and the United Kingdom, convened an Arria Formula meeting of the UN Security Council to address the implications of the proliferation and misuse of commercial spyware for international peace and security, as well as for upholding human rights and fundamental freedoms. The meeting further affirmed the U.S. commitment to work in partnership with likeminded Member States, as well as representatives of civil society, industry, and victims of commercial spyware abuse to confront these challenges.
U.S. leadership under the Biden-Harris Administration has cemented a strong international recognition of the acute threat posed by the misuse of commercial spyware to international security. Through today’s meeting, the United States seeks to advance dialogue and initiatives to develop and implement policies to discourage the misuse of commercial spyware and encourage the development and implementation of responsible use principles that are consistent with respect for universal human rights, the rule of law, and civil rights and civil liberties. In this regard, the United States welcomed Slovenia’s announcement at today’s Arria to join the Joint Statement on Efforts to Counter the Proliferation and Misuse of Commercial Spyware.
This marks the third Arria Formula session on emerging technology and cyber issues convened by the Biden-Harris Administration. It builds on the United States’ leadership at the UN in addressing commercial spyware threats, including the adoption of a landmark U.S.-led Human Rights Council resolution recognizing the dangers posed by the misuse of commercial spyware to democratic values, human rights, and fundamental freedoms. This milestone followed a U.S.-hosted meeting on the margins of the UN General Assembly in September, further solidifying international attention on this pressing issue.
“It is clear there is broad consensus including here at the UN regarding the threat that commercial spyware poses to peace and security, human rights, and sustainable development,” said Deputy Permanent Representative Dorothy Shea. “We look forward to discussing what more we can do together to further protect our citizens, as well as international peace and security, from the misuse and proliferation of commercial spyware.”
