Ambassador Dorothy Shea
Deputy Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
January 13, 2025
Remarks at a UN Security Council Arria-formula Meeting on “Violations of international humanitarian law against Ukrainian prisoners of war and civilian detainees”
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam Chair. And I’d like to thank Mr. Pablo de Greiff from the International Commission of Inquiry on Ukraine and also honor our other briefers: Valerii Horishnii, Nariman Dzhelyal, and Maksym Butkevych for sharing your own harrowing experiences in Russian captivity and also what you observed.
Madam Chair, as today’s briefers remind us, the number of independent and credible reports by international observers are overwhelming in detailing the inhumane treatment of Ukrainian civilian detainees and prisoners of war by Russia.
The Commission of Inquiry on Ukraine, in its most recent report, found that Russia’s use of torture against detained Ukrainians has been “widespread and systematic,” amounting to a “coordinated State policy.” The Commission concluded Russian authorities had committed the crime against humanity of torture in the context of their full-scale invasion of Ukraine.
Evidence of Russia’s crimes are available to us thanks to the essential reporting from the Commission of Inquiry, the UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission in Ukraine, the OSCE’s Moscow Mechanism Experts Mission and the Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights, Ukrainian civil society organizations, and a variety of NGOs and other outside experts.
The evidence is overwhelming. Their reports document Russia’s abuses against detained Ukrainians, to include physical, psychological, and sexual torture. The United States has credible evidence that Russia systematically holds detainees incommunicado, often without access to adequate food, sanitation, or medical services. We have credible evidence detainees face inhumane treatment and abuse for displaying any form of national pride or for refusing to swear allegiance to Russia.
The United States assesses Russian forces continue to regularly torture and kill Ukrainian POWs and we have seen an increase in reports of Ukrainian POW executions over the past year. In September and October alone, we estimate Russian troops executed 40 Ukrainian prisoners of war along the front line in Ukraine and in Kursk Oblast. The executions include shooting surrendering Ukrainian forces at close range and the brutality of beheadings and dismemberments.
Madam Chair, I fear we run the risk of becoming desensitized to these revelations. For years we have heard unrelenting testimony of Russia’s shocking abuses and atrocities. We must not lose sight of how reprehensible and unacceptable they are.
So long as Russia continues this course, the international community must not do “business as usual” with the Russian Federation. The United States will continue to support Ukraine, and to hold Russia and Russian officials to account for their unlawful actions.
We commend the members of the Security Council who have endeavored to hold Russia to account for its actions. Speaking up in this forum on behalf of the UN Charter and international humanitarian law demonstrates global leadership and makes the world safer.
We have also heard some members of the Council, however, make statements that provide Russia with political cover. Such statements enable Russia’s transgressions and make it difficult to take these members seriously as global leaders.
As Ukrainians fight to defend their country and their families from the horrors of Russia’s crimes, telling Ukrainians to “meet Russia half-way” or “take seriously Russia’s legitimate security concerns” is absurd, and it is wrong. A victim should never be told to meet their aggressor half-way.
There is no equivalency in this conflict. Russia is the aggressor, Ukraine its victim.
So, let us not engage in false equivalency. The Russian Federation must immediately cease its war of choice and its ill-treatment of prisoners of war and civilian detainees, including torture and summary executions. Russia must hear – and heed – our calls to allow international humanitarian organizations access to POWs and civilian detainees, consistent with its obligations under the Geneva Conventions.
The United States condemns Russia’s appalling abuses against POWs and detained civilians, which strike at the heart of the United Nation’s mission of protecting the people of the world from the scourge of war. As UN Security Council members, we must insist on Ukraine’s sovereignty and territorial integrity.
We must work together to bring Russia’s unlawful invasion to an end and to support Ukraine in its pursuit of a comprehensive, just, and sustainable peace consistent with UN Charter principles.
Let me underscore that any peace must adhere to the principle of “nothing about Ukraine without Ukraine.” To be sustainable, peace must fully respect Ukraine’s territorial integrity within its internationally recognized borders, as well as Ukraine’s sovereignty and political independence.
I thank you, Madam Chair.
